#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139583} :activity/id #uuid "958bf76d-900f-5728-a665-cbbaccc0d8c0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048566 :topic/id #uuid "aa5df21b-533b-51c0-b02d-90918cede430" :topic/math-academy-id 2356} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139703 :tutorial/content "Often, we can subtract mixed numbers in a similar way to adding them. Let's remind ourselves of the steps:\n\n> **Step 1**: We subtract whole numbers from whole numbers,\n> **Step 2**: Then, we subtract fractions from fractions, converting to equivalent fractions with a common denominator when necessary.\n> **Step 3**: We combine the results from steps 1 and 2.\n\nLet's use this method to find the value of\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac{3}{4} - 2\\,\\dfrac 1 8\n$$\n\n**Step 1**: Subtracting the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n3-2 = {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now, we subtract the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} - \\dfrac 1 8\n$$\n\nHere, we can make a common denominator of $8$. To put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 4\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $8$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{3\\times 2}{4\\times 2} = \\dfrac{6}{8}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same and we subtract the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{8} - \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{5}{8}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We now combine our results to get the final answer:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}1} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 8}} = {\\color{red}{1}}\\,{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 8}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f94a7ba4-c6be-565c-aec5-11441b7bef13" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8593 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "254938fe-8ac6-59c7-a5f1-479e3b277348" :step/math-academy-id 96487 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138575 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138139 :question/id #uuid "8f412bc3-e3fb-56da-8ded-e201ca5086c5" :question/math-academy-id "e-13767"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a938039c-93b3-59e8-b917-ad1b9aa8f2f9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050637}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139072}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting Two Mixed Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "bc7e9ba9-e6f1-5a37-b839-e89ad252c700" :step/math-academy-id 96488 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139585}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138576 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138140 :question/id #uuid "6227ddee-c908-57ca-aaaf-c39fe162b4db" :question/math-academy-id "e-13769"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58ea7610-365c-5fee-8e8f-3d6fd68cd196" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139181}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Whole Number Part Equals Zero"} :step/id #uuid "e58386f1-1d63-53dd-b912-2fdacf8eab19" :step/math-academy-id 96491 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139586}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138577 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138141 :question/id #uuid "0f46d710-abf6-5fb0-a305-9b2a023555d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13770"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c87e441-a534-5f6b-82e7-34e2ef8b9639" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050444}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445319}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting Two Mixed Numbers Using the Least Common Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "dc1f6285-6037-5159-bf65-12e7d5ce6582" :step/math-academy-id 96494}] :activity/title "Subtracting Mixed Numbers With Unlike Denominators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138656}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074833} :activity/id #uuid "3507f4d5-37a7-54b2-b585-b7a0486bae42" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048953 :topic/id #uuid "ceb546c9-9534-5144-80c0-2b6b1f2c9b23" :topic/math-academy-id 541} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074833 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074956 :tutorial/content "We can use box models to divide numbers by two-digit numbers.\n\nLet's see how to use a box model by finding the value of $300 \\div 25$.\n\nWe start by writing our division in the following way. We place the dividend in a box and the divisor to the left of the box.\n\n|  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $25$ | $3$$0$$0$ |  |  |\n\nWe want to find how many times $25$ goes into $300$. But instead of trying to figure this out in a single step, let's break this problem up into a series of simpler steps.\n\nIn our first step, we will pick a multiple of $25$ that is easy to compute and is fairly large but no larger than $300$. We will choose the multiple $25 \\times {\\color{blue}10}$ because we can compute\n\n$$\n25 \\times {\\color{blue}10} = 250\n$$\n\nquickly and the result is no larger than $300$.\n\nTo organize our work so far, we write $\\color{blue}10$ above the box and subtract $25 \\times {\\color{blue}10}$ from $300$ inside the box.\n\n|  | $\\color{blue}10$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $25$ | $3$$0$$0$$-\\!$$2$$5$$0$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$ |  |  |\n\nThe result of the subtraction is ${\\color{red}50}$. This indicates that if we take $25$ a total of ${\\color{blue}10}$ times, then it will fit into $300$ with $\\color{red}50$ left over.\n\nNow, we repeat the process with a dividend of $\\color{red}50$ instead of $300$. To start the next round, we write $\\color{red}50$ in a new box to the right.\n\n|  | $10$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $25$ | $3$$0$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$5$$0$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$ | $\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$ |  |\n\nAgain, we are looking for a multiple of $25$ that is easy to compute and is no larger than ${\\color{red}50}$. We will choose the multiple $25 \\times {\\color{blue}2}$ because we can compute\n\n$$\n25 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 50\n$$\n\nquickly and the result is no larger than ${\\color{red}50}$.\n\nAs before, we write $\\color{blue}2$ above the box and subtract $25 \\times {\\color{blue}2}$ from ${\\color{red}50}$ inside the box.\n\n|  | $10$ | $\\color{blue}2$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $25$ | $3$$0$$0$$-\\!$$2$$5$$0$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$ | $\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |  |\n\nWe get $\\fbox{0}$, so the division is done.\n\nTo find the quotient $300 \\div 25$, all we need to do is add the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n10+2=12\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n300 \\div 25 = 12\\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ea1605db-abbe-5c50-acc7-74e45ebbde3f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1876 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a0ad99e3-627e-5c90-8760-6ca05b23450e" :step/math-academy-id 6395 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074834}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074834 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073856 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073535 :question/id #uuid "9b1d9b9c-0b04-5659-9833-04cfc2282ea3" :question/math-academy-id "e-1842"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35a065c8-d6c8-5504-be2e-842683907bef" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049278}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074354}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Box Method to Divide a Three-Digit Number Using Two Boxes"} :step/id #uuid "7fe41f66-e205-5ce6-b076-f286231ceea9" :step/math-academy-id 6396 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074835}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074835 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073857 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073536 :question/id #uuid "e75c155f-cbc0-58ce-a188-970f7af70948" :question/math-academy-id "e-1843"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff8c499-d050-5b9e-bfe8-8802f88d3af6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051073}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074358}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Box Method to Divide a Three-Digit Number Using Three Boxes"} :step/id #uuid "b72e2a19-0ea5-5162-85ec-bde05d693c0e" :step/math-academy-id 6397 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074836}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074836 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073858 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073537 :question/id #uuid "b3c9bedb-4e90-530a-95e0-ceafca5a749c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1844"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5daaf6-f575-521f-bf44-227bf6de13ea" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048924}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445317}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Box Method to Divide a Four-Digit Number Using Three Boxes"} :step/id #uuid "4b385f80-95e4-57b0-9413-8befcde59dd9" :step/math-academy-id 6398}] :activity/title "Multi-Digit by Two-Digit Division Using Partial Quotients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073961}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139200} :activity/id #uuid "452ce2a1-c927-58b6-ad75-838c81a9de93" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049142 :topic/id #uuid "de1ba9e7-b383-53c4-925a-41d277478966" :topic/math-academy-id 2289} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139710 :tutorial/content "Remember that to *multiply* a decimal by $10$, we shift the decimal point one space to the *right*.\n\nSo, to *divide* a decimal by $10$, we shift the decimal point one place to the *left*.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's use this method to find the value of\n\n$$\n38.7 \\div 10\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down our number. We can attach as many leading zeros as we want to the whole number part:\n\n![](images/5b/5bac3fcfdb0abdb1550b3802da497b48eadc5389bd381f02a8fbbc4adca73391.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $10$. Since $10$ contains $1$ zero, we move the decimal point $1$ place to the left:\n\n![](images/c9/c9d94def2d8d7d8c52fd05ec0a50fd04cb2f755cc7ef242d727f6c4632444685.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/4f/4f4096b1615e8da780e3b6f7f68d76a0eb62057c4b79e9bbca9439feb3f88435.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n$3.87$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n38.7 \\div 10 = 3.87\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0eea9a29-cf7c-5439-8a35-5d5f914448ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8660 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "33b796f4-d434-599d-962b-290bddde9ff0" :step/math-academy-id 103062 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139201}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139201 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138329 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138155 :question/id #uuid "135fa171-6d8d-508b-96b9-edca29e2aa54" :question/math-academy-id "e-13889"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91fb4b28-4839-575a-94ad-e9733650d129" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049747}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445320}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Number by Ten"} :step/id #uuid "c3e88ea6-07ad-57f2-ac92-4e32887987ae" :step/math-academy-id 103063 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139202}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139202 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138330 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138158 :question/id #uuid "3b80fc87-9f27-5653-986c-eb2305eb2b5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13895"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388265c-8b83-5afc-a9f2-595b2b349a44" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139153}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Number Smaller Than Ten by Ten"} :step/id #uuid "a703a35c-dceb-52a9-8995-98d4d66d4a50" :step/math-academy-id 103066 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139203}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139712 :tutorial/content "To divide a decimal by a larger power of $10$, we shift the decimal point to the left by one place for each zero in the power of $10$.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use this method to find the value of\n\n$$\n9 \\div 100\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down our number. We can attach as many trailing zeros as we want to the whole number part.\n\n![](images/45/459955cbc7618333d1069460b3195918578e2b46b9afbffaddf719de10738aca.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $100$. Since $100$ contains $2$ zeros, we move the decimal point $2$ places to the left:\n\n![](images/69/69d5b5414b7b481d598ddde52a87e256f9e16b0e2ca83dcd62eb67fbcf948362.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/cc/ccef2b026c6f4567dd56ebed3a03fd2da0dcc3680aa4a750d5f56de4511f6dab.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n\n$$\n0.090 = 0.09\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n9 \\div 100 = 0.09\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e21ac71f-a163-5cb3-ac9d-6788ab646ecb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8663 :tutorial/title "Dividing Decimals by Larger Powers of Ten"} :step/id #uuid "1c976a79-9212-511d-b1a8-1f024eae937a" :step/math-academy-id 103069 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139204}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139204 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138331 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138159 :question/id #uuid "2f9cf814-a9ab-5ab3-bd9a-6a6bcfde0b0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13898"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf10eb38-9207-5cf2-a631-8e97fdf199b3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138965}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Number by One Hundred"} :step/id #uuid "31551500-82a9-5963-bb78-3c3d3cff17c8" :step/math-academy-id 103070 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139205}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139205 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138332 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138160 :question/id #uuid "8176b084-c620-51ce-89d7-ccde8e9c1dd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13899"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21be25e3-728a-5d4d-ae5d-4a4ce89886cf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138679}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Number by One Thousand"} :step/id #uuid "0bb937ac-cf72-5664-aec7-bc6818c40abd" :step/math-academy-id 103073}] :activity/title "Dividing Decimals by Powers of Ten Using Decimal Point Placement" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138611}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064983} :activity/id #uuid "ff4dacd2-616f-5c37-aea4-06ce3f316305" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049549 :topic/id #uuid "fea55e34-a154-51c1-a822-7bdbcc1b3a68" :topic/math-academy-id 326} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065443 :tutorial/content "Adding decimals is no harder than adding whole numbers.\n\nFor example, let's find $0.4 + 2.6$.\n\nFirst, we write one number above the other, lining up the decimal points and placing a decimal point below the others in our answer:\n\n$$\n0\\begin{vmatrix}. & 4 \\\\ + & 2\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 6;\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers. After each addition, we write the ones place and carry the tens place of the result.\n\n$$\n1; 0\\begin{vmatrix}. & 4 \\\\ + & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.4 + 2.6 = 3\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4187b0b6-fac2-59b0-8079-0fe059d96b55" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8919 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f8af6c07-74c5-50cf-b043-5026a198fc89" :step/math-academy-id 106958 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064984}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064984 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064155 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063994 :question/id #uuid "bfcdf621-dba2-57ab-98d6-69556699f2a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-14408"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1498fdbc-4309-5f23-94d6-821c4c7cbddb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048844}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064910}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding Two-Digit and Three-Digit Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "d024f2ef-600e-541e-804b-27c5080558cb" :step/math-academy-id 106959 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064985}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064156 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063996 :question/id #uuid "c26881da-6d1f-5973-a312-24647570ee23" :question/math-academy-id "e-14411"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0872095f-fb0d-506e-a2c1-110854bd5d16" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048556}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064501}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding Decimals Containing Different Numbers of Whole Digits"} :step/id #uuid "4c71c3b6-34f8-5e50-a394-3ee6e80bee02" :step/math-academy-id 106962 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064986}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064986 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064157 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063997 :question/id #uuid "3151a337-7dae-5e2d-9c0f-f5d0a3954e89" :question/math-academy-id "e-14414"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03e83caf-cbcb-506c-b726-b4bfe3c057be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049256}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064912}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding Four-Digit, Five-Digit and Six-Digit Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "b17a6e08-b7ff-5320-87ea-6395d26f68ab" :step/math-academy-id 106965 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064987}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064987 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064158 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063995 :question/id #uuid "c0112e09-65d1-5b25-bb6a-cce2ad0a8fbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-14409"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d20bb661-d55b-5db1-a353-a6d088ed3f86" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445318}] :knowledge-point/title "Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "d48da9f7-60c8-5994-ab02-eeb0ce2487f2" :step/math-academy-id 106968}] :activity/title "Adding Decimals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064417}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139273} :activity/id #uuid "1348192c-c5f4-5718-84e1-0e6228776fb7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050131 :topic/id #uuid "2f4d3750-cf40-5c57-8c23-75261a047653" :topic/math-academy-id 2353} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139699 :tutorial/content "How do we find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{{\\color{red}{3}}} + \\dfrac 1 {\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nwithout using a fraction model?\n\nThe denominators are $\\color{red}3$ and $\\color{blue}6$, which are not the same. So to add the fractions, we need to find a common denominator.\n\nNotice that ${\\color{blue}{6}}$ is a multiple of ${\\color{red}{3}}$. The multiples of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ are:\n\n$$\n\\quad 3, {\\color{blue}{6}}, 9, 12, \\ldots\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least common denominator is ${\\color{blue}{6}}$.\n\nWe can put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $\\color{blue}6$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{red}{3}} = \\dfrac{2\\times2}{{\\color{red}{3}}\\times 2} = \\dfrac{4}{\\color{blue}{6}}\n$$\n\nWe don't need to do anything to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nsince it already has a denominator of ${\\color{blue}6}$.\n\nNow, we can add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer!\n\nWe would have gotten the same answer using a fraction model, and the method is very similar:\n\n![](images/81/8162bf953ef140eaf9deb692474c5c76b83903de3216c81ac03dc714850cf25e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "7e26409b-aaa1-52cb-b859-643c607bc0db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8579 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "653ab556-ac85-53df-9366-dd87ed3d05c9" :step/math-academy-id 134958 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139274}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139274 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138375 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138132 :question/id #uuid "6b6c9042-15ac-5a0e-8398-68e7b712c245" :question/math-academy-id "e-13738"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "593c1987-90c4-5796-9495-efd6ef4a70a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050462}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139048}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding Fractions When One Denominator is a Multiple of the Other"} :step/id #uuid "1590b647-48b6-549e-9979-e9b3eb563f55" :step/math-academy-id 134959 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139275}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139275 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139700 :tutorial/content "When adding fractions, if one denominator is not a multiple of the other, we can always find a common denominator by multiplying the denominators together.\n\nFor example, let's find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{red}2}+\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\color{red}2$ is not a multiple of ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ and vice-versa. However, an easy common denominator is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2}\\times {\\color{blue}3} = 6\n$$\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}3}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{{\\color{red}{2}}} = \\dfrac{1\\times {\\color{blue}{3}}}{{\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}}} = \\dfrac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{red}{2}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{{\\color{blue}{3}}} = \\dfrac{1\\times {\\color{red}{2}}}{{\\color{blue}{3}} \\times {\\color{red}{2}}} = \\dfrac{2}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{6} + \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nNotice that the fraction model gives us the same result:\n\n![](images/f2/f2704054fbbc29cf499a0f4c1293f79073e614070472fed4d29042d093e07ebf.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6e218979-ac1b-54d9-96aa-977cb17e1bf3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8580 :tutorial/title "Finding a Common Denominator by Multiplying the Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "306890ff-e120-5250-80b0-44fe5cfefaa3" :step/math-academy-id 134963 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139276}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139276 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138376 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138133 :question/id #uuid "428ab0f7-87e6-5ca7-8808-e0c5a7763c9f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13739"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "521266c7-b308-5641-98f3-351d1ce63d0e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138949}] :knowledge-point/title "Making a Common Denominator by Multiplying the Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "d75a8f5a-bdca-5e5c-b8c7-03c87bfa5381" :step/math-academy-id 134964 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139277}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139701 :tutorial/content "In the last few examples, we made a common denominator by multiplying the denominators. Sometimes, this means that we need to compute a large product, which we'd like to avoid if we can.\n\nWhen two denominators have a common factor, it's sometimes easier to find the lowest common multiple, rather than multiplying.\n\nFor example, let's add the two fractions\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nNotice that the denominators ${6}$ and ${8}$ have a common factor of $2$. So let's look at their multiples:\n\n- Multiples of $6: \\quad 6,12,18,{\\color{blue}{24}},30,\\ldots$\n- Multiples of $8: \\quad 8,16,{\\color{blue}{24}},32,40,\\ldots$\n\nThe lowest common multiple is ${\\color{blue}{24}}$. This is the lowest common denominator, too.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 5 6\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $24$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6} = \\dfrac{5\\times 4}{6 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{20}{24}\n$$\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 8\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $24$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{8} = \\dfrac{3\\times 3}{8 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{9}{24}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{24} + \\frac{9}{24} = \\frac{29}{24}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9ea81051-d8f6-5494-bcbf-f96bdf982ac8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8581 :tutorial/title "Adding Fractions Using the Least Common Multiple"} :step/id #uuid "a78448b8-9f3c-57bc-81b7-fe2af685978e" :step/math-academy-id 134968 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139278}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138377 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138134 :question/id #uuid "a61790a4-96d3-5346-b83e-8f70858ee3b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13740"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05636e74-7815-5a97-92a0-e29c2804d419" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445321}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding Fractions Using the Least Common Multiple"} :step/id #uuid "1cc6952f-a2ad-539f-9ccf-c9621c7f6433" :step/math-academy-id 134969}] :activity/title "Adding Fractions With Unlike Denominators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138653}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143686} :activity/id #uuid "49acebb3-e5bb-53c3-a854-dacc58b65725" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050610 :topic/id #uuid "589205b6-43f1-59a1-a894-6d8e8438cfcd" :topic/math-academy-id 2381} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143686 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144084 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to multiply the following mixed numbers:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3} \\times 1\\,\\dfrac 2{5}\n$$\n\nTo solve this problem, we can use the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Convert both mixed numbers to improper fractions. \n\n- First, we write $1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 1)+1}{3}= \\dfrac43\n$$\n\n- We also write $1\\,\\dfrac25$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac25=\\dfrac{(5\\times 1)+2}{5}= \\dfrac75\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Multiply the fractions by multiplying the numerators and denominators separately:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac43\\times\\dfrac75=  \\dfrac {4\\times 7} {3\\times 5} = \\dfrac{28}{15}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Simplify or convert back to a mixed number, if necessary:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{28}{15} = 1 \\, \\text{R} 13 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{13}{15}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $1 \\, \\dfrac{13}{15}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0435613c-64c1-5d4e-88c5-4feb68728088" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8628 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1819f7b8-f4b6-511f-bd23-27cecff8194c" :step/math-academy-id 136323 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142809 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142620 :question/id #uuid "68ab3d23-d9c7-5037-9ff5-1803bee39a02" :question/math-academy-id "e-13826"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f481bacc-75b7-525d-8784-9e5138117597" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050927}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435288}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Result as an Improper Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "c44b63ab-267b-51fd-ae51-98b9bc741ffb" :step/math-academy-id 136324 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143688}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142810 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142621 :question/id #uuid "1130bb19-8742-53c5-b07c-22c310f1d927" :question/math-academy-id "e-13829"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8e4d9a7-afea-562f-b013-9c02fbaf3455" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435292}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Result is a Whole Number"} :step/id #uuid "685aa823-a785-5324-9fd3-e3d962ee3081" :step/math-academy-id 136327 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143689}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142623 :question/id #uuid "b93f9bde-7033-529a-a8fa-63066c95d48e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13834"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0261453-d071-5dbf-8d63-39c77ab39c72" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445322}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Result as a Mixed Number"} :step/id #uuid "9df1a603-b707-553c-b92d-bf9263e6eb8b" :step/math-academy-id 136330 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143690}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142812 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142625 :question/id #uuid "1dcc8e5a-21ab-52af-b9ef-fccb97d74eb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13838"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6340c1e2-bf12-5932-9632-df7b72f499d9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435290}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Result as a Mixed Number (Solution Uses a Strategy)"} :step/id #uuid "60085969-f43a-5066-ae55-5742c042e9d1" :step/math-academy-id 136333}] :activity/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186143016}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139212} :activity/id #uuid "6e61060a-d40d-5e87-8579-445884ade10a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051030 :topic/id #uuid "7bb4d253-9922-5e90-95bf-ab6cf0e0d7aa" :topic/math-academy-id 2347} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139212 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139708 :tutorial/content "To multiply a decimal by a multi-digit whole number, such as $28 \\times 6.2$, we can use the usual method of removing and replacing the decimal point.\n\n**Step 1.** First, ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}4[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; 2\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}.2; 5\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 3\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Then, we insert the decimal point to have the same number of decimal places as the decimal factor.\n\nHere, there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $6.2$, so the product will also have ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ decimal place. We take our value of $1736$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place:\n\n$$\n1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n28 \\times 6.2 = 173.6 \\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ee38f29a-637d-5f43-a28c-cd3e1588f024" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8655 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "73b03df3-0141-5bba-85ed-4872281bdbb6" :step/math-academy-id 108049 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139213}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139213 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138337 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138149 :question/id #uuid "e66187e6-2d74-5900-88a2-8c58c7f84902" :question/math-academy-id "e-13874"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0be5d51-f93c-56e1-8d4f-6b8b7a73f1c7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050007}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138945}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Decimal With One Decimal Place by a Two-Digit Whole Number"} :step/id #uuid "140e2f0a-b2b8-5151-91be-a0a396bfddf3" :step/math-academy-id 108050 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139214}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139214 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138338 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138150 :question/id #uuid "d27175bf-0f82-5870-b340-1abb4a01fe0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-13876"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "684b0d42-6052-5c33-a6ee-5e9971e50657" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050042}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139024}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Decimal With One Decimal Place by a Three-Digit Whole Number"} :step/id #uuid "d187d9ff-4250-50ca-a768-94a5fa541ca3" :step/math-academy-id 108053 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139215}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139215 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138339 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138151 :question/id #uuid "93225702-b473-5499-92e8-2b2724ac0b4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13879"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad3dcc6-12db-5990-b9ff-07ea3db27c14" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048374}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139023}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Decimal With Two Decimal Places by a Three-Digit Whole Number"} :step/id #uuid "af783892-b41b-533e-98d2-f308d8225752" :step/math-academy-id 108056 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139216}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139216 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138340 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138152 :question/id #uuid "230545ff-4eef-5447-9e0f-2cbd0c3e6f4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-13882"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c331171-b536-55c6-92d9-6d22b9200a05" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048844}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445316}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Decimal by a Whole Number: Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "1aec48c9-02ad-5d44-a602-117f6518ba5a" :step/math-academy-id 108059}] :activity/title "Multiplying a Decimal by a Multi-Digit Whole Number" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138647}]}
